A. Every year millions of people give their time to charities without being paid. Researchers have asked why they do it, and their answers fall into three main groups.

B. The first group wants to help others. A volunteer at a food bank, for example, said that handing out meals made her feel useful. The second group wants to learn new skills, such as organising events or speaking to the public.

C. The third group simply wants company. Retired people often say that volunteering gives them a reason to leave the house and meet friends. Charities therefore try to offer jobs that suit all three groups.', 'VALID');
